WEBVTT

1
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.416
We’re here at the Bitcoin Conference.

2
00:00:44.416 --> 00:00:46.041
And today, it’s such an honor.

3
00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:49.708
Because we have the founder of Nano Labs, Kong Jianping.

4
00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:51.083
Mr. Jack Kong is here.

5
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:51.583
Hello.

6
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:52.583
Hello, nice to meet you.

7
00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:54.750
Stablecoins are being talked about a lot.

8
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.583
But outsiders often look at stablecoins and say,

9
00:00:57.583 --> 00:00:59.666
isn’t it just numbers on online banking?

10
00:00:59.666 --> 00:01:01.083
And then you say it’s on-chain,

11
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:03.125
trying to make it look fancy, right?

12
00:01:03.125 --> 00:01:05.375
But for regular people,

13
00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:08.791
how should they really understand stablecoins?

14
00:01:08.791 --> 00:01:11.583
Understanding stablecoins is actually very simple.

15
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:12.625
A stablecoin,

16
00:01:12.625 --> 00:01:14.166
is basically fiat currency.

17
00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:17.250
It’s just fiat currency that exists on a blockchain.

18
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.166
In this sense,

19
00:01:18.166 --> 00:01:21.458
in the digital world people use stablecoins,

20
00:01:21.458 --> 00:01:23.500
while in the traditional world they use fiat.

21
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.291
So by itself, stablecoins don’t have investment value.

22
00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:29.916
But because the digital world has many quality assets,

23
00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:33.083
they might provide higher returns than traditional ones.

24
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:35.666
So for stablecoins—

25
00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:36.666
many people hold them,

26
00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:38.666
and it really solves two problems.

27
00:01:38.666 --> 00:01:40.000
First,

28
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.750
much higher efficiency in global transfers and payments.

29
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:44.291
Second,

30
00:01:44.291 --> 00:01:47.416
participating in all kinds of digital asset investments.

31
00:01:47.416 --> 00:01:48.375
Including DeFi,

32
00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:49.875
including quant strategies,

33
00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:53.333
and all these activities that add to TVL.

34
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:55.125
But now with the U.S. Genius Act,

35
00:01:55.125 --> 00:01:55.791
people think,

36
00:01:55.791 --> 00:01:58.541
isn’t this just America’s way of exporting U.S. debt?

37
00:01:58.541 --> 00:02:00.708
A new method, right?

38
00:02:00.708 --> 00:02:02.041
Before it was the petrodollar,

39
00:02:02.041 --> 00:02:04.791
and now it’s the stablecoin dollar.

40
00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.333
Yeah, I think so.

41
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:10.958
Trump pushing the Genius Act so aggressively,

42
00:02:10.958 --> 00:02:14.041
at its core, is to solve the U.S. debt problem.

43
00:02:14.041 --> 00:02:14.833
You think so too, right?

44
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.041
And at the same time,

45
00:02:16.041 --> 00:02:20.958
it indirectly achieves a greater internationalization of the dollar,

46
00:02:20.958 --> 00:02:22.416
strengthening dollar hegemony,

47
00:02:22.416 --> 00:02:24.333
and ultimately,

48
00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:26.833
ensures financial innovation happens in the U.S.

49
00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:30.375
So do you think Hong Kong or other regions

50
00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:32.416
should also issue their own stablecoins?

51
00:02:32.416 --> 00:02:34.333
In theory, I think

52
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:36.166
every fiat should have a stablecoin.

53
00:02:36.166 --> 00:02:38.291
But in practice,

54
00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:41.541
the digital world—the whole Web3 world—

55
00:02:41.541 --> 00:02:43.125
is more like the internet.

56
00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:47.291
Which means the head effect will be very strong.

57
00:02:47.291 --> 00:02:49.708
In the past, because of nation-states,

58
00:02:49.708 --> 00:02:51.916
politics, and those concepts,

59
00:02:51.916 --> 00:02:55.291
we had the dollar, euro, renminbi, yen,

60
00:02:55.291 --> 00:02:59.125
and many other mainstream fiat currencies

61
00:02:59.125 --> 00:03:03.208
participating in the global system.

62
00:03:03.208 --> 00:03:07.541
But I think in the future, the dollar has the best chance,

63
00:03:07.541 --> 00:03:09.625
and second might be the renminbi.

64
00:03:09.625 --> 00:03:11.500
An offshore RMB stablecoin.

65
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.875
Domestically it’s just the onshore RMB,

66
00:03:14.875 --> 00:03:17.166
and abroad it’s the offshore RMB stablecoin.

67
00:03:17.166 --> 00:03:20.000
So when I take it out, it becomes offshore, right?

68
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
What you take out usually can’t really go abroad.

69
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.541
So where do the funds that go overseas come from?

70
00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:29.166
From overseas trade,

71
00:03:29.166 --> 00:03:30.416
settlement, global business,

72
00:03:30.416 --> 00:03:33.125
transactions between China and other countries.

73
00:03:33.125 --> 00:03:37.083
That’s what generates them.

74
00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.625
I’ve heard people say,

75
00:03:38.625 --> 00:03:41.083
you know how in crypto, settlements now

76
00:03:41.083 --> 00:03:42.333
are all in USD stablecoins.

77
00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:45.208
So the U.S. has already won this so-called

78
00:03:45.208 --> 00:03:47.541
cryptocurrency war.

79
00:03:47.541 --> 00:03:49.750
And other countries really didn't need to play along.

80
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.625
It was too far off.

81
00:03:50.625 --> 00:03:51.916
Actually, the U.S. didn't win on its own.

82
00:03:51.916 --> 00:03:53.166
China helped it win.

83
00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:54.458
Yes — and why is that?

84
00:03:54.458 --> 00:03:57.500
Because stablecoins originally weren't very useful.

85
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.791
The first wave happened inside China.

86
00:04:00.791 --> 00:04:03.125
Some things emerged on the mainland.

87
00:04:03.125 --> 00:04:06.875
Ministry-level documents said Bitcoin is a virtual commodity.

88
00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:08.041
Ordinary people could freely buy and sell it.

89
00:04:08.041 --> 00:04:10.583
But financial institutions were not allowed to participate.

90
00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:11.708
Financial institutions were not allowed to participate.

91
00:04:11.708 --> 00:04:14.166
Back then, over 90% of global trading volume

92
00:04:14.166 --> 00:04:17.833
was on Chinese exchanges, right?

93
00:04:17.833 --> 00:04:19.250
At that time, Coinbase and others

94
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:20.708
weren't as big as they are today.

95
00:04:20.708 --> 00:04:25.125
At the same time, 70–80% of mined Bitcoin

96
00:04:25.125 --> 00:04:27.833
was also mined by Chinese miners.

97
00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:30.750
And 100% of mining rig production

98
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.750
was manufactured in mainland China at the time.

99
00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.416
So China controlled the largest

100
00:04:37.416 --> 00:04:39.958
global demand for digital currency trading

101
00:04:39.958 --> 00:04:41.875
and Bitcoin production.

102
00:04:41.875 --> 00:04:44.250
Then China proactively banned

103
00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.750
RMB-to-crypto trading.

104
00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.250
US dollar stablecoins seized that opportunity

105
00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.916
and moved in. Yes.

106
00:04:50.916 --> 00:04:51.666
And at that time,

107
00:04:51.666 --> 00:04:54.583
it became the first use-case for dollar stablecoins.

108
00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:57.208
Look at Circle's IPO — it was very hot.

109
00:04:57.208 --> 00:04:58.291
In their prospectus,

110
00:04:58.291 --> 00:05:00.916
most of the profit went to exchanges.

111
00:05:00.916 --> 00:05:01.708
To Coinbase,

112
00:05:01.708 --> 00:05:02.916
to Binance, right?

113
00:05:02.916 --> 00:05:04.750
So in that scenario,

114
00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.875
using USDT on exchanges didn't require paying the exchange.

115
00:05:06.875 --> 00:05:08.708
Because exchanges needed it too, right?

116
00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.583
Timing is crucial for startups.

117
00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:11.250
Yes, right?

118
00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.375
So the first wave was China's policy

119
00:05:15.375 --> 00:05:16.708
pushing the whole

120
00:05:16.708 --> 00:05:19.791
USD stablecoin momentum to its first peak.

121
00:05:19.791 --> 00:05:22.000
The exchange-use case was confirmed.

122
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.625
You could buy Bitcoin via Taobao,

123
00:05:23.625 --> 00:05:25.291
by making payments

124
00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:28.083
to the exchange's RMB account.

125
00:05:28.083 --> 00:05:29.500
Deposit into the exchange.

126
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.708
So that was the first reason

127
00:05:31.708 --> 00:05:33.125
USD stablecoins took off.

128
00:05:33.125 --> 00:05:34.791
The second reason was

129
00:05:34.791 --> 00:05:35.958
after Ethereum rose up,

130
00:05:35.958 --> 00:05:37.375
the whole DeFi ecosystem formed.

131
00:05:37.375 --> 00:05:38.791
In the DeFi world,

132
00:05:38.791 --> 00:05:40.958
you need a stable price

133
00:05:40.958 --> 00:05:43.458
to enable investment in those projects,

134
00:05:43.458 --> 00:05:45.083
to participate in TVL,

135
00:05:45.083 --> 00:05:47.375
and to interact with various DeFi protocols.

136
00:05:47.375 --> 00:05:49.166
That was the second use-case for USD stablecoins.

137
00:05:49.166 --> 00:05:52.458
So now the Ethereum ecosystem accounts for

138
00:05:52.458 --> 00:05:54.458
almost half of the

139
00:05:54.458 --> 00:05:56.000
USD stablecoin circulation.

140
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Yes. And the third was the global

141
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.208
scenarios shunned by traditional finance,

142
00:06:02.208 --> 00:06:03.166
like payments,

143
00:06:03.166 --> 00:06:04.583
including gambling and other illicit activities, right?

144
00:06:04.583 --> 00:06:06.416
Various payment scenarios and flows.

145
00:06:06.416 --> 00:06:10.333
Also cross-border transfers are more efficient with stablecoins.

146
00:06:10.333 --> 00:06:13.625
So that's another payment-related use-case globally.

147
00:06:13.625 --> 00:06:15.958
In some countries, fiat is unstable,

148
00:06:15.958 --> 00:06:18.125
so people switch to stablecoins

149
00:06:18.125 --> 00:06:20.750
to achieve more stability.

150
00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:23.750
So globally, payment-related uses are another scenario.

151
00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.083
These are the three main categories.

152
00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:27.041
So these are the three big use-cases.

153
00:06:27.041 --> 00:06:28.208
Can I understand it that way?

154
00:06:28.208 --> 00:06:30.375
That in the past, most of Bitcoin's hashpower

155
00:06:30.375 --> 00:06:31.875
was in China,

156
00:06:31.875 --> 00:06:34.666
and as you said, Ethereum and others

157
00:06:34.666 --> 00:06:36.166
everyone went to China to raise funds.

158
00:06:36.166 --> 00:06:37.333
So at that time,

159
00:06:37.333 --> 00:06:40.083
if China had launched its own stablecoin,

160
00:06:40.083 --> 00:06:42.166
we might not be using USDT today.

161
00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:44.541
Right. If China had supported the industry back then

162
00:06:44.541 --> 00:06:48.166
and settled in RMB,

163
00:06:48.166 --> 00:06:50.083
USD stablecoins wouldn't be so strong.

164
00:06:50.083 --> 00:06:53.750
Then maybe in the future the renminbi could surpass the dollar

165
00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:54.833
and become the largest

166
00:06:54.833 --> 00:06:57.166
in the digital asset world.

167
00:06:57.166 --> 00:06:58.250
Do you think that's a pity?

168
00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.625
I don't think it's a pity. Why?

169
00:06:59.625 --> 00:07:02.083
Because China emphasizes

170
00:07:02.083 --> 00:07:03.666
preventing financial risks.

171
00:07:03.666 --> 00:07:07.041
So these very early-stage financial innovations

172
00:07:07.041 --> 00:07:09.125
simply don't fit into China's existing

173
00:07:09.125 --> 00:07:12.083
industrial-focused system.

174
00:07:12.083 --> 00:07:14.125
Such an innovation

175
00:07:14.125 --> 00:07:15.791
Hong Kong values financial stability,

176
00:07:15.791 --> 00:07:18.875
so its stablecoin policy will be, compared to others,

177
00:07:18.875 --> 00:07:20.666
a bit more inclined toward

178
00:07:20.666 --> 00:07:22.958
protecting investors,

179
00:07:22.958 --> 00:07:24.875
anti-money laundering, and similar measures.

180
00:07:24.875 --> 00:07:26.541
So Hong Kong is like that.

181
00:07:26.541 --> 00:07:28.583
The U.S. wants to ensure financial innovation.

182
00:07:28.583 --> 00:07:30.916
It has always used finance

183
00:07:30.916 --> 00:07:32.625
to influence the world.

184
00:07:32.625 --> 00:07:35.166
So all financial innovation must happen in the U.S.,

185
00:07:35.166 --> 00:07:37.333
otherwise the country would decline, right?

186
00:07:37.333 --> 00:07:40.916
So these three regions have different systems and DNA.

187
00:07:40.916 --> 00:07:42.041
They're just different.

188
00:07:42.041 --> 00:07:45.333
So this thing couldn't happen in China.

189
00:07:45.333 --> 00:07:48.833
It's a more cultural, deep-rooted idea.

190
00:07:48.833 --> 00:07:50.166
If it were Hong Kong,

191
00:07:50.166 --> 00:07:52.625
Hong Kong is also a financial hub,

192
00:07:52.625 --> 00:07:54.333
a very important place.

193
00:07:54.333 --> 00:07:58.166
You mentioned being worried about some risks —

194
00:07:58.166 --> 00:08:00.083
what exactly are those risks?

195
00:08:00.083 --> 00:08:02.458
Because once stablecoins are on-chain,

196
00:08:02.458 --> 00:08:03.875
regulation becomes quite difficult.

197
00:08:03.875 --> 00:08:06.083
Because they're decentralized,

198
00:08:06.083 --> 00:08:07.791
and they can interact across different chains,

199
00:08:07.791 --> 00:08:09.250
even cross-chain.

200
00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:11.375
So money laundering,

201
00:08:11.375 --> 00:08:12.875
and various illegal financial activities,

202
00:08:12.875 --> 00:08:15.166
can be conducted via stablecoins,

203
00:08:15.166 --> 00:08:16.833
or after using stablecoins,

204
00:08:16.833 --> 00:08:20.833
you can't control them the way you can in the banking system.

205
00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:22.458
So can I view stablecoins as

206
00:08:22.458 --> 00:08:23.625
just a tool?

207
00:08:23.625 --> 00:08:26.250
And you might use it to commit crimes.

208
00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.791
The tool itself is neutral,

209
00:08:28.791 --> 00:08:29.916
but what you use it for...

210
00:08:29.916 --> 00:08:31.166
maybe I use it to do good,

211
00:08:31.166 --> 00:08:32.916
or someone might use it to do bad.

212
00:08:32.916 --> 00:08:37.291
Do you think, as a government,

213
00:08:37.291 --> 00:08:41.416
I should regulate this neutral tool?

214
00:08:41.416 --> 00:08:43.083
Here’s how I see it.

215
00:08:43.083 --> 00:08:44.291
I think this thing—

216
00:08:44.291 --> 00:08:45.791
stablecoins—definitely have

217
00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:47.083
huge value for society.

218
00:08:47.083 --> 00:08:49.541
They’re not neutral; they’re a good thing.

219
00:08:49.541 --> 00:08:51.500
But as you said,

220
00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.041
most people use a kitchen knife to cut food,

221
00:08:54.041 --> 00:08:56.833
but a few use it to kill, right?

222
00:08:56.833 --> 00:08:58.458
Stablecoins are the same.

223
00:08:58.458 --> 00:09:02.041
So I think governments should actively promote this.

224
00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:04.250
Don’t worry about the negatives stablecoins bring;

225
00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.125
be proactive and push them forward.

226
00:09:06.125 --> 00:09:08.916
Along the way, keep patching the problems as they appear.

227
00:09:08.916 --> 00:09:09.541
Right?

228
00:09:09.541 --> 00:09:11.291
You can’t patch everything upfront

229
00:09:11.291 --> 00:09:12.916
and then lock everyone in a dark room to work.

230
00:09:12.916 --> 00:09:14.250
How would that even work, right?

231
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.541
So it should be like: in a controlled venue you can experiment freely, right?

232
00:09:17.541 --> 00:09:18.916
In this building you can do anything.

233
00:09:18.916 --> 00:09:20.166
In this place you can try things out.

234
00:09:20.166 --> 00:09:21.458
Then when problems are found,

235
00:09:21.458 --> 00:09:24.166
you add legal patches one by one, right?

236
00:09:24.166 --> 00:09:25.333
Because it’s too new, right?

237
00:09:25.333 --> 00:09:27.333
Actually, I recently heard a story

238
00:10:28.791 --> 00:10:30.125
about this Bitcoin Conference.

239
00:10:30.125 --> 00:10:31.750
Eric Trump was here, you know.

240
00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:33.541
He’s Trump’s son.

241
00:10:33.541 --> 00:10:36.791
When they tried to persuade him to come, they said,

242
00:10:36.791 --> 00:10:39.541
“Do you want to come to the biggest Bitcoin conference in Asia?”

243
00:10:39.541 --> 00:10:41.166
And he said,

244
00:10:41.166 --> 00:10:43.208
“Is there a Bitcoin community in Asia?”

245
00:10:43.208 --> 00:10:44.916
When I heard that I got goosebumps.

246
00:10:44.916 --> 00:10:47.250
Because actually most of the hashpower

247
00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.375
used to be in China.

248
00:10:49.375 --> 00:10:53.500
And now everyone talks about “Wall Street Bitcoin,”

249
00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.666
as if

250
00:10:55.666 --> 00:10:58.250
it’s something Wall Street can just speculate on.

251
00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:00.583
Hearing it that way,

252
00:11:00.583 --> 00:11:03.000
do you feel a bit bitter?

253
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:03.666
Definitely.

254
00:11:03.666 --> 00:11:06.291
I entered this industry in 2012, you know.

255
00:11:06.291 --> 00:11:09.041
And the world’s first

256
00:11:09.041 --> 00:11:10.708
Bitcoin mining chip

257
00:11:10.708 --> 00:11:15.291
was designed by my company, Canaan.

258
00:11:15.291 --> 00:11:18.208
I spent four or five years

259
00:11:18.208 --> 00:11:20.333
taking that company public.

260
00:11:20.333 --> 00:11:21.833
It listed in the U.S. in 2019.

261
00:11:21.833 --> 00:11:23.458
Becoming the world’s first stock in this field.

262
00:11:23.458 --> 00:11:25.791
Back then there were basically no companies in this sector.

263
00:11:25.791 --> 00:11:27.166
Canaan was the first to go public.

264
00:11:27.166 --> 00:11:29.416
China was leading the world at that time.

265
00:11:29.416 --> 00:11:30.125
Yes.

266
00:11:30.125 --> 00:11:31.375
In the internet era,

267
00:11:31.375 --> 00:11:32.791
China used to copy others,

268
00:11:32.791 --> 00:11:35.750
but in the mobile internet era China progressed alongside.

269
00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.791
And in the blockchain/Web3 era,

270
00:11:37.791 --> 00:11:40.708
China was leading the world then, right?

271
00:11:40.708 --> 00:11:43.250
Then gradually China started issuing policies

272
00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:45.750
that classified mining as to be phased out.

273
00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.041
So why did the U.S. benefit?

274
00:11:47.041 --> 00:11:48.958
Because China is such a large country,

275
00:11:48.958 --> 00:11:51.041
there are many different voices.

276
00:11:51.041 --> 00:11:52.250
Some policies may be good,

277
00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:53.875
and some, in hindsight,

278
00:11:53.875 --> 00:11:56.291
might have caused harm to the country and its people, right?

279
00:11:56.291 --> 00:11:57.291
I think this policy

280
00:11:57.291 --> 00:11:59.041
definitely caused

281
00:11:59.041 --> 00:12:02.416
irreparable damage to the country’s future, right?

282
00:12:02.416 --> 00:12:04.625
Because how could mining be classified as obsolete, right?

283
00:12:04.625 --> 00:12:06.833
Bitcoin can earn you U.S. dollars, right?

284
00:12:06.833 --> 00:12:10.958
And it can use surplus power in underdeveloped regions—

285
00:12:10.958 --> 00:12:14.125
unused wind, solar, and other electricity resources.

286
00:12:14.125 --> 00:12:15.833
That benefits the country and its people.

287
00:12:15.833 --> 00:12:17.333
It’s just that in the process,

288
00:12:17.333 --> 00:12:19.041
if someone uses it illegally,

289
00:12:19.041 --> 00:12:20.375
you should regulate them.

290
00:12:20.375 --> 00:12:21.875
But the thing itself

291
00:12:21.875 --> 00:12:24.583
is very valuable to the country, right?

292
00:12:24.583 --> 00:12:26.666
Because it doesn’t waste the country’s resources.

293
00:12:26.666 --> 00:12:30.041
It turns large amounts of resources into money for the country.

294
00:12:30.041 --> 00:12:31.458
Right? Sell it to Wall Street.

295
00:12:31.458 --> 00:12:33.291
So if you look,

296
00:12:33.291 --> 00:12:34.416
after that policy,

297
00:12:34.416 --> 00:12:36.375
the first batch of U.S. companies were doing what?

298
00:12:36.375 --> 00:12:37.208
Mining.

299
00:12:37.208 --> 00:12:37.666
Yes.

300
00:12:37.666 --> 00:12:39.916
So companies like Marathon and RIOT

301
00:12:39.916 --> 00:12:42.791
rose on the U.S. stock market.

302
00:12:42.791 --> 00:12:45.666
Originally these should have been Chinese companies, right?

303
00:12:45.666 --> 00:12:49.375
But because of China’s policy, these companies

304
00:12:49.375 --> 00:12:51.541
became U.S. companies that far outpaced Chinese ones.

305
00:12:51.541 --> 00:12:52.375
Because they’re in the U.S.,

306
00:12:52.375 --> 00:12:56.041
they understand the U.S. infrastructure better, right?

307
00:12:56.041 --> 00:12:57.791
Now this cycle is different.

308
00:12:57.791 --> 00:12:59.625
This time it’s DAT token stocks.

309
00:12:59.625 --> 00:13:02.166
Meaning those mining companies

310
00:13:02.166 --> 00:13:04.125
had to buy Chinese mining rigs,

311
00:13:04.125 --> 00:13:06.625
procure Chinese materials to build farms,

312
00:13:06.625 --> 00:13:09.041
and were influenced by Chinese industry.

313
00:13:09.041 --> 00:13:11.875
But now these DAT token strategies

314
00:13:11.875 --> 00:13:13.750
raise capital directly on Wall Street,

315
00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:15.083
and with the funds they buy coins directly,

316
00:13:15.083 --> 00:13:18.500
without needing any mainland China resources involved,

317
00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.250
right? Unless you invest in them.

318
00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.166
So their growth speed

319
00:13:22.166 --> 00:13:24.625
far exceeds the growth rate of the previous mining cycle.

320
00:13:24.625 --> 00:13:26.416
In the last cycle, for example,

321
00:13:26.416 --> 00:13:30.000
companies like those miners and Canaan

322
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.333
at IPO had market caps

323
00:13:31.333 --> 00:13:33.583
in the billions of dollars, right?

324
00:13:33.583 --> 00:13:34.916
But this cycle is different.

325
00:13:34.916 --> 00:13:37.000
In this cycle, leading U.S. company MSTR

326
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.125
by buying coins has become a company worth hundreds of billions of dollars.

327
00:13:39.125 --> 00:13:42.041
China now — even the biggest companies in China or Hong Kong

328
00:13:42.041 --> 00:13:43.541
are only worth a few hundred million dollars, right?

329
00:13:43.541 --> 00:13:46.041
So that opens up a gap of more than two orders of magnitude.

330
00:13:46.041 --> 00:13:47.125
So I think

331
00:13:47.125 --> 00:13:49.708
this round it’s the U.S. companies

332
00:13:49.708 --> 00:13:53.416
that really pull far ahead of Chinese companies.

333
00:13:53.416 --> 00:13:54.708
In the last round everyone was similar,

334
00:13:54.708 --> 00:13:57.083
but this round these new U.S. companies

335
00:13:57.083 --> 00:13:59.166
or the old U.S. coin-holding companies

336
00:13:59.166 --> 00:14:01.958
have greatly widened the gap with China.

337
00:14:03.916 --> 00:14:06.083
Speaking of mining, I want to give an example:

338
00:14:06.083 --> 00:14:09.791
miners are a bit like developers building a building.

339
00:14:09.791 --> 00:14:11.125
Because Michael Saylor says

340
00:14:11.125 --> 00:14:13.125
Bitcoin is digital real estate, right?

341
00:14:13.125 --> 00:14:16.000
Digital real estate.

342
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.041
So if miners are the builders,

343
00:14:19.041 --> 00:14:22.000
then these DAT companies

344
00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.666
are directly buying the land.

345
00:14:23.666 --> 00:14:25.208
Because the thing that will appreciate

346
00:14:25.208 --> 00:14:27.708
is the land itself, not the operation.

347
00:14:27.708 --> 00:14:30.875
The building, right. Yes.

348
00:14:30.875 --> 00:14:32.583
So who will still mine in the end?

349
00:14:32.583 --> 00:14:34.625
So right now,

350
00:14:34.625 --> 00:14:36.250
the market values mining low

351
00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:38.541
for that reason.

352
00:14:38.541 --> 00:14:39.208
Because buying coins directly is simpler than mining.

353
00:14:39.208 --> 00:14:41.416
Yes, it’s more efficient.

354
00:14:41.416 --> 00:14:42.583
But not necessarily cheaper—

355
00:14:42.583 --> 00:14:44.750
it’s just simpler.

356
00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:45.875
Right, it’s just simpler.

357
00:14:45.875 --> 00:14:47.250
Because mining involves a whole set of operations

358
00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:49.208
and also factory production.

359
00:14:49.208 --> 00:14:50.500
If you don’t manage it well,

360
00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:53.083
your farm can lose power,

361
00:14:53.083 --> 00:14:54.291
your rigs can be stolen,

362
00:14:54.291 --> 00:14:56.458
and for various reasons

363
00:14:56.458 --> 00:14:57.416
there are operational risks.

364
00:14:57.416 --> 00:14:59.333
So the market says you might as well just buy coins directly.

365
00:14:59.333 --> 00:15:02.000
MSTR’s model is simpler.

366
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.083
But people still mine — why?

367
00:15:04.083 --> 00:15:06.250
Because compared to traditional businesses,

368
00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:07.916
mining still

369
00:15:07.916 --> 00:15:10.083
has a relatively quick payback cycle.

370
00:15:10.083 --> 00:15:11.458
Now, Bitcoin mining

371
00:15:11.458 --> 00:15:13.375
may pay back in 300 to 500 days

372
00:15:13.375 --> 00:15:14.833
in static conditions.

373
00:15:14.833 --> 00:15:17.458
Dynamically it can change—

374
00:15:17.458 --> 00:15:18.416
coin prices change, hashrate changes.

375
00:15:18.416 --> 00:15:20.666
So compared to traditional industries that take 3–5 years

376
00:15:20.666 --> 00:15:22.416
or even 5–10 years to break even,

377
00:15:22.416 --> 00:15:25.291
mining is still pretty attractive.

378
00:15:25.291 --> 00:15:27.791
So can I understand that

379
00:15:27.791 --> 00:15:29.125
because if you can mine,

380
00:15:29.125 --> 00:15:30.583
you could also run a Bitcoin treasury company,

381
00:15:30.583 --> 00:15:32.041
so why not do that directly?

382
00:15:32.041 --> 00:15:34.583
Because mining is relatively simpler;

383
00:15:34.583 --> 00:15:35.875
you just need money to buy rigs.

384
00:15:35.875 --> 00:15:38.708
A Bitcoin treasury company now requires much larger scale.

385
00:15:38.708 --> 00:15:42.000
You need players of at least a billion dollars

386
00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.416
to participate.

387
00:15:43.416 --> 00:15:45.416
Even if you have ten million dollars you can play

388
00:15:45.416 --> 00:15:47.375
and be a listed company,

389
00:15:47.375 --> 00:15:48.166
but for a Bitcoin treasury—

390
00:15:48.166 --> 00:15:50.583
a company with ten million in Bitcoin

391
00:15:50.583 --> 00:15:52.333
won’t get attention.

392
00:15:52.333 --> 00:15:53.125
No market recognition,

393
00:15:53.125 --> 00:15:54.958
no credibility.

394
00:15:54.958 --> 00:15:56.208
Nano Labs implemented a BNB strategy.

395
00:15:56.208 --> 00:15:58.250
Tell us about that strategy.

396
00:15:56.208 --> 00:15:58.250
The market has no recognition at all.

397
00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:00.166
And it also has no credibility.

398
00:16:00.166 --> 00:16:04.750
Nano Labs implemented a BNB strategy.

399
00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:06.250
Talk about that strategy.

400
00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:07.958
Simply put, because I myself actually

401
00:16:07.958 --> 00:16:09.791
primarily hold Bitcoin.

402
00:16:09.791 --> 00:16:11.791
Our family office has invested in many

403
00:16:11.791 --> 00:16:13.916
different DAT-listed companies.

404
00:16:13.916 --> 00:16:18.000
And Nano Labs, as our publicly listed holding company,

405
00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.625
at the time thought DAT would be a future trend.

406
00:16:20.625 --> 00:16:22.500
So listed companies holding coins is a trend.

407
00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.333
The coins to hold would be Bitcoin,

408
00:16:24.333 --> 00:16:26.291
Ethereum, Solana, BNB, right?

409
00:16:26.291 --> 00:16:28.625
So maybe four or five in total.

410
00:16:28.625 --> 00:16:30.583
At that point we chose BNB.

411
00:16:30.583 --> 00:16:32.958
Because Bitcoin already had a very large scale.

412
00:16:32.958 --> 00:16:34.291
If you went after it then,

413
00:16:34.291 --> 00:16:37.708
it wouldn’t be so easy to make a market impact.

414
00:16:37.708 --> 00:16:39.083
As for Ethereum,

415
00:16:39.083 --> 00:16:42.000
there were already one or two companies accumulating it.

416
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:43.666
I talked with CZ at the time,

417
00:16:43.666 --> 00:16:45.291
and I said, “Why don’t we accumulate BNB?”

418
00:16:45.291 --> 00:16:46.458
He thought it was a good idea.

419
00:16:46.458 --> 00:16:50.791
So Nano Labs began strategic reserves of BNB.

420
00:16:50.791 --> 00:16:53.708
BNB has some advantages.

421
00:16:53.708 --> 00:16:55.291
For example, when new tokens are issued,

422
00:16:55.291 --> 00:16:57.041
it’s Binance’s platform token.

423
00:16:57.041 --> 00:16:58.333
That means

424
00:16:58.333 --> 00:17:00.708
if your goal is to have

425
00:17:00.708 --> 00:17:03.291
5–10% of BNB in your company’s holdings, right?

426
00:17:03.291 --> 00:17:04.916
Actually let me correct something first.

427
00:17:04.916 --> 00:17:06.416
BNB is no longer Binance’s platform token.

428
00:17:06.416 --> 00:17:08.333
BNB is now a decentralized token. Got it.

429
00:17:08.333 --> 00:17:13.041
But it can still enjoy some yields from Binance Launchpad.

430
00:17:13.041 --> 00:17:15.250
Those bring certain returns.

431
00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:17.166
So its yields have been around

432
00:17:17.166 --> 00:17:20.458
approximately a 12% annualized rate over the past six months.

433
00:17:20.458 --> 00:17:23.666
But we don’t know what the future price will be.

434
00:17:23.666 --> 00:17:25.041
Right, so it does generate yields.

435
00:17:25.041 --> 00:17:27.750
Why do people say it’s no longer a platform token?

436
00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:29.750
Because now,

437
00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.833
BNB has undergone fundamental changes.

438
00:17:33.833 --> 00:17:35.750
Right — in the early days

439
00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:38.458
it might have been a token issued when Binance was founded.

440
00:17:38.458 --> 00:17:40.541
But now it is not Binance’s platform token anymore.

441
00:17:40.541 --> 00:17:43.875
Do you think that’s because it has grown so much?

442
00:17:43.875 --> 00:17:44.916
So no.

443
00:17:44.916 --> 00:17:47.500
Or has its fundamentals changed in some way?

444
00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:50.083
It’s like Binance is just one of the

445
00:17:50.083 --> 00:17:52.708
important parties in the BNB ecosystem

446
00:17:52.708 --> 00:17:56.416
providing part of the enabling support for it.

447
00:17:56.416 --> 00:17:57.875
Right. OK.

448
00:17:57.875 --> 00:18:00.000
But okay, if it is an enabling party,

449
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.541
and part of its enablement provides new tokens

450
00:18:02.541 --> 00:18:04.625
to those on the Launchpad,

451
00:18:04.625 --> 00:18:06.166
the returns from those other tokens,

452
00:18:06.166 --> 00:18:08.291
are counted as company revenue too.

453
00:18:08.291 --> 00:18:09.791
Right.

454
00:18:09.791 --> 00:18:11.000
Interesting.

455
00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.791
Ethereum had just come out,

456
00:18:14.791 --> 00:18:17.041
and you said when you were considering,

457
00:18:17.041 --> 00:18:18.958
there were already one or two companies with Ethereum.

458
00:18:18.958 --> 00:18:22.083
That is actually rare in various industries —

459
00:18:22.083 --> 00:18:24.166
only one or two players is very few.

460
00:18:24.166 --> 00:18:25.333
But like I said,

461
00:18:25.333 --> 00:18:28.791
the more top-tier something is, the stronger its liquidity.

462
00:18:28.791 --> 00:18:33.000
So it gets more attention and better brand recognition.

463
00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.208
Okay — so I want to ask:

464
00:18:35.208 --> 00:18:36.666
What will your strategy be?

465
00:18:36.666 --> 00:18:38.833
Is it to never sell?

466
00:18:38.833 --> 00:18:41.250
Is it the same as those Bitcoin treasury companies?

467
00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:43.375
Personally, I used to say

468
00:18:43.375 --> 00:18:46.208
in 2013, in my earliest video interview on "Yangyang Interviews,"

469
00:18:46.208 --> 00:18:47.166
when he interviewed me,

470
00:18:47.166 --> 00:18:49.166
I said, don't sell Bitcoin for ten years.

471
00:18:49.166 --> 00:18:50.625
Indeed, over the past decade

472
00:18:50.625 --> 00:18:53.833
apart from investing in companies where investment was necessary,

473
00:18:53.833 --> 00:18:56.166
we basically haven't sold Bitcoin for personal reasons.

474
00:18:56.166 --> 00:18:57.625
We haven't sold Bitcoin.

475
00:18:57.625 --> 00:19:00.208
So yes, we've been long-term bullish on Bitcoin

476
00:19:00.208 --> 00:19:02.125
and the value of mainstream digital currencies.

477
00:19:02.125 --> 00:19:03.916
Currently the company holds BNB,

478
00:19:03.916 --> 00:19:05.375
and the strategy is also not to sell.

479
00:19:05.375 --> 00:19:07.583
But running a public company

480
00:19:07.583 --> 00:19:08.791
is different from doing it personally.

481
00:19:08.791 --> 00:19:12.083
So the market will fluctuate

482
00:19:12.083 --> 00:19:15.208
and there will be different changes over the development.

483
00:19:15.208 --> 00:19:17.541
We judge based on how the market is at the time.

484
00:19:17.541 --> 00:19:19.333
But for now our strategy is definitely not to sell.

485
00:19:19.333 --> 00:19:21.958
What advice would you give ordinary people?

486
00:19:21.958 --> 00:19:23.500
Also to not sell mainstream coins?

487
00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:26.916
For regular people, just buy Bitcoin.

488
00:19:26.916 --> 00:19:29.375
Don't touch other tokens.

489
00:19:29.375 --> 00:19:30.916
Why?

490
00:19:30.916 --> 00:19:31.750
Because

491
00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:34.875
Bitcoin is the leader in this sector.

492
00:19:34.875 --> 00:19:37.333
Other coins like BNB, Ethereum, Solana

493
00:19:37.333 --> 00:19:39.458
require analysis and judgment

494
00:19:39.458 --> 00:19:41.375
to determine which are good or bad.

495
00:19:41.375 --> 00:19:43.583
Of course we think BNB is pretty good,

496
00:19:43.583 --> 00:19:47.458
but that approach is for professional institutional investors.

497
00:19:47.458 --> 00:19:48.250
We feel that

498
00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:50.250
we've been in this industry for over a decade

499
00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:51.750
and understand the coins better.

500
00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.875
So besides Bitcoin we would buy BNB,

501
00:19:53.875 --> 00:19:55.958
but as an ordinary investor

502
00:19:55.958 --> 00:19:58.125
buying Bitcoin is simpler.

503
00:19:58.125 --> 00:19:58.833
No need to worry.

504
00:19:58.833 --> 00:20:01.041
It's like if you bought Tencent stock years ago—

505
00:20:01.041 --> 00:20:03.416
you just hold it, right?

506
00:20:03.416 --> 00:20:06.333
Many people talk about Bitcoin's four-year cycle, right?

507
00:20:06.333 --> 00:20:08.333
Usually when we look at historical peaks,

508
00:20:08.333 --> 00:20:11.625
they occur 12–18 months after a Bitcoin halving.

509
00:20:11.625 --> 00:20:13.333
If that's true,

510
00:20:13.333 --> 00:20:17.125
then 18 months would be October this year.

511
00:20:17.125 --> 00:20:20.208
This is like "The Three-Body Problem"—have you seen it?

512
00:20:20.208 --> 00:20:23.791
It talks about turkeys and the farmer, right?

513
00:20:23.791 --> 00:20:26.458
I think this analysis is the "turkey analyst" type.

514
00:20:26.458 --> 00:20:28.166
How so?

515
00:20:28.166 --> 00:20:29.208
Why?

516
00:20:29.208 --> 00:20:32.375
People thought Bitcoin had a four-year cycle

517
00:20:32.375 --> 00:20:34.916
because Bitcoin halves every four years.

518
00:20:34.916 --> 00:20:36.416
Right.

519
00:20:36.416 --> 00:20:37.666
But actually this halving now

520
00:20:37.666 --> 00:20:39.875
has very, very little impact on Bitcoin.

521
00:20:39.875 --> 00:20:42.000
Because daily issuance is already very small.

522
00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.000
Halving it again doesn't change much.

523
00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.916
Given the current fundamentals at just over $20k,

524
00:20:46.916 --> 00:20:48.250
the impact is very small, right?

525
00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:51.000
So that’s where this analysis comes from.

526
00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.625
Everyone says history shows it that way, right?

527
00:20:53.625 --> 00:20:56.833
But the future won't necessarily follow that.

528
00:20:56.833 --> 00:20:58.250
The future will be different.

529
00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.416
In the past, each big Bitcoin crash was an 80%–90% drawdown.

530
00:21:01.416 --> 00:21:03.791
But such huge crashes won't happen again.

531
00:21:03.791 --> 00:21:06.458
Because more and more traditional capital

532
00:21:06.458 --> 00:21:10.666
and long-term funds are allocating to Bitcoin.

533
00:21:10.666 --> 00:21:12.916
So they won't sell off in such huge volumes.

534
00:21:12.916 --> 00:21:14.500
For example, Bitcoin may behave like

535
00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.916
top U.S. stocks,

536
00:21:16.916 --> 00:21:18.958
with drawdowns of maybe 30–40%.

537
00:21:18.958 --> 00:21:23.458
U.S. stocks have generally been in a bull market over the past decade-plus.

538
00:21:23.458 --> 00:21:25.041
Overall it's been a bull market.

539
00:21:25.041 --> 00:21:27.666
Each bear market is just a short period.

540
00:21:27.666 --> 00:21:28.541
Right?

541
00:21:28.541 --> 00:21:30.083
Not like Bitcoin's old four-year cycles.

542
00:21:30.083 --> 00:21:30.916
Four-year cycles.

543
00:21:30.916 --> 00:21:32.125
So I think in the future

544
00:21:32.125 --> 00:21:34.875
Bitcoin will be closer to the behavior of top U.S. stocks.

545
00:21:34.875 --> 00:21:36.708
The current state of leading U.S. stocks.

546
00:21:36.708 --> 00:21:40.375
But its performance will likely be even better than those top U.S. stocks.

547
00:21:40.375 --> 00:21:42.708
That means its volatility will decrease,

548
00:21:42.708 --> 00:21:44.208
and the upside might also shrink.

549
00:21:44.208 --> 00:21:45.041
No.

550
00:21:45.041 --> 00:21:47.833
Its drawdowns will be smaller, but gains could increase.

551
00:21:47.833 --> 00:21:48.500
How so?

552
00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:52.541
Like Nvidia's big gains over the past two years, right?

553
00:21:52.541 --> 00:21:53.875
And two years earlier,

554
00:21:53.875 --> 00:21:55.541
Tesla had huge gains.

555
00:21:55.541 --> 00:21:57.250
It's not that once it reaches hundreds of billions

556
00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:58.291
it can't rise anymore.

557
00:21:58.291 --> 00:21:59.958
Precisely because it reached hundreds of billions,

558
00:21:59.958 --> 00:22:01.875
more funds will allocate to it.

559
00:22:01.875 --> 00:22:04.000
For example, once it's over $500 billion,

560
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:06.541
pension funds can buy digital assets, right?

561
00:22:06.541 --> 00:22:08.708
Right now Bitcoin is the only investable digital asset.

562
00:22:08.708 --> 00:22:10.375
So globally,

563
00:22:10.375 --> 00:22:12.625
U.S. stocks have the trait that the bigger the stock,

564
00:22:12.625 --> 00:22:15.083
the better the top-tier stocks tend to perform.

565
00:22:15.083 --> 00:22:17.958
Smaller stocks, conversely, can't rally as much, right?

566
00:22:17.958 --> 00:22:20.583
Of course some exceptional stocks do take off,

567
00:22:20.583 --> 00:22:21.958
but overall speaking,

568
00:22:21.958 --> 00:22:23.041
if you speculate on small caps

569
00:22:23.041 --> 00:22:24.666
you might be better off holding a few large ones.

570
00:22:24.666 --> 00:22:25.833
The same goes for Bitcoin.

571
00:22:25.833 --> 00:22:28.250
Speculating on altcoins may be worse than just holding Bitcoin.

572
00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.458
So Bitcoin’s upside certainly won’t be small.

573
00:22:30.458 --> 00:22:31.750
And if Bitcoin doesn’t go up,

574
00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:32.875
the other coins won’t either.

575
00:22:32.875 --> 00:22:34.750
Many will say,

576
00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:37.208
“Okay, if I’m a very professional,

577
00:22:37.208 --> 00:22:39.541
a skilled trader,

578
00:22:39.541 --> 00:22:41.750
I’ll trade altcoins,

579
00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:42.958
chase meme coins,

580
00:22:42.958 --> 00:22:45.583
or go after inscriptions (ordinals).”

581
00:22:45.583 --> 00:22:48.083
Make some money and then buy Bitcoin.

582
00:22:48.083 --> 00:22:53.041
But right now that strategy has been utterly losing in this cycle.

583
00:22:53.041 --> 00:22:53.916
This cycle—

584
00:22:53.916 --> 00:22:56.500
yes, so look at inscriptions: there were 10x, 100x, 1000x moves.

585
00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:58.291
But in the long run,

586
00:22:58.291 --> 00:23:01.416
one, they can’t absorb much capital;

587
00:23:01.416 --> 00:23:03.083
two, their drawdowns are brutal—often 90%,

588
00:23:03.083 --> 00:23:05.833
even 99.9%, right?

589
00:23:05.833 --> 00:23:08.041
So unless you’re very lucky—

590
00:23:08.041 --> 00:23:09.958
and heavily held an ORDI

591
00:23:09.958 --> 00:23:11.333
and sold it above $90—

592
00:23:11.333 --> 00:23:13.791
otherwise if you bought in at $20–30

593
00:23:13.791 --> 00:23:17.916
and it ran to $90 and you didn’t sell,

594
00:23:17.916 --> 00:23:19.791
it might only be $8–9 now, right?

595
00:23:19.791 --> 00:23:22.000
So for most people,

596
00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:24.875
Bitcoin is the simplest investment tool.

597
00:23:24.875 --> 00:23:27.541
Can I understand the Bitcoin ecosystem —

598
00:23:27.541 --> 00:23:30.125
because many guests discuss that

599
00:23:30.125 --> 00:23:32.416
these DATs

600
00:23:32.416 --> 00:23:34.125
or many Bitcoin companies

601
00:23:34.125 --> 00:23:36.166
need to generate cash flow,

602
00:23:36.166 --> 00:23:38.541
and Bitcoin needs to be put to work,

603
00:23:38.541 --> 00:23:40.708
put into DeFi,

604
00:23:40.708 --> 00:23:41.791
to be lent,

605
00:23:41.791 --> 00:23:42.833
are those adjacent projects

606
00:23:42.833 --> 00:23:45.166
things worth paying attention to?

607
00:23:45.166 --> 00:23:46.583
Theoretically speaking,

608
00:23:46.583 --> 00:23:47.791
if you ensure the Bitcoin isn’t stolen,

609
00:23:47.791 --> 00:23:50.166
having Bitcoin participate in various DeFi TVL

610
00:23:50.166 --> 00:23:52.666
I think is fine.

611
00:23:52.666 --> 00:23:54.125
And it can bring positive cash flow to a company;

612
00:23:54.125 --> 00:23:56.750
even using Bitcoin for quant strategies

613
00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:58.041
can generate positive cash flow.

614
00:23:58.041 --> 00:23:59.916
I think those are all OK.

615
00:23:59.916 --> 00:24:01.708
But you must pay attention to one thing:

616
00:24:01.708 --> 00:24:03.708
whether the Bitcoin can be stolen.

617
00:24:03.708 --> 00:24:04.791
Or when Bitcoin goes into DeFi,

618
00:24:04.791 --> 00:24:06.291
it becomes a wrapped asset.

619
00:24:06.291 --> 00:24:08.291
What that means is,

620
00:24:08.291 --> 00:24:11.958
because Bitcoin isn’t natively compatible with Ethereum,

621
00:24:11.958 --> 00:24:12.583
you essentially

622
00:24:12.583 --> 00:24:15.541
need to insert (wrap) Bitcoin into the EVM ecosystem

623
00:24:15.541 --> 00:24:16.708
or other DeFi ecosystems,

624
00:24:16.708 --> 00:24:19.708
and use that wrapped Bitcoin to participate.

625
00:24:19.708 --> 00:24:22.291
In that case, the wrapped Bitcoin being stolen

626
00:24:22.291 --> 00:24:24.083
doesn’t necessarily affect the original directly.

627
00:24:24.083 --> 00:24:25.500
But because your Bitcoin then circulates in the market,

628
00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.500
you have to handle redemption for everyone holding it.

629
00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:31.208
So if a theft happens and those stolen coins are picked up by different retail holders,

630
00:24:31.208 --> 00:24:34.083
when those retail holders come to redeem, that’s a risk.

631
00:24:34.083 --> 00:24:36.166
Given how bullish people are on Bitcoin now,

632
00:24:36.166 --> 00:24:39.750
can you think of the most dangerous scenario?

633
00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:41.958
What would be the biggest risk?

634
00:24:41.958 --> 00:24:46.000
I think the biggest theoretical risk is a 51% attack.

635
00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:46.875
Theoretically,

636
00:24:46.875 --> 00:24:49.791
if you control 51% of global Bitcoin mining hashpower,

637
00:24:49.791 --> 00:24:50.875
you could attack the chain.

638
00:24:50.875 --> 00:24:53.583
But it’s unlikely to happen.

639
00:24:53.583 --> 00:24:55.708
Personally, I think existing participants

640
00:24:55.708 --> 00:24:58.083
have no real incentive to do that,

641
00:24:58.083 --> 00:25:00.916
because it would also hurt them.

642
00:25:00.916 --> 00:25:03.500
Their own Bitcoin holdings would become worth less.

643
00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.708
First, that action would be bad for them personally

644
00:25:05.708 --> 00:25:07.833
—unless they were heavily shorting, right?

645
00:25:07.833 --> 00:25:09.083
Otherwise, Bitcoin rising

646
00:25:09.083 --> 00:25:11.708
is the foundation for everyone’s gains.

647
00:25:11.708 --> 00:25:12.708
Unless they short massively.

648
00:25:12.708 --> 00:25:14.750
Secondly, now there are ETFs

649
00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:16.250
and many mainstream funds,

650
00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:18.958
sovereign funds and pension funds are participating.

651
00:25:18.958 --> 00:25:22.458
If you deliberately and organizedly launch a 51% attack,

652
00:25:22.458 --> 00:25:25.500
in the native crypto world that raises the “code is law” question,

653
00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:28.125
but in the real world, is that illegal?

654
00:25:28.125 --> 00:25:29.541
It isn’t.

655
00:25:55.583 --> 00:25:57.083
I think it could be considered so.

656
00:25:57.083 --> 00:25:58.208
Because why?

657
00:25:58.208 --> 00:26:01.291
Because you would have caused destruction to society, right?

658
00:26:01.291 --> 00:26:02.291
Who would catch you?

659
00:26:02.291 --> 00:26:04.500
Because you attacked that chain,

660
00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:08.166
everyone's assets suffered losses, right?

661
00:26:08.166 --> 00:26:09.791
Isn't that a kind of financial crime?

662
00:26:09.791 --> 00:26:11.250
But it's decentralized,

663
00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:12.916
there's no central authority.

664
00:26:12.916 --> 00:26:16.208
So it's like your stablecoin being decentralized too,

665
00:26:16.208 --> 00:26:17.958
but in the real world

666
00:26:17.958 --> 00:26:19.833
regulators can still supervise you.

667
00:26:19.833 --> 00:26:21.291
KYC is required,

668
00:26:21.291 --> 00:26:24.458
and law enforcement can freeze your stablecoins.

669
00:26:24.458 --> 00:26:25.458
It's basically the same.

670
00:26:25.458 --> 00:26:27.750
So if in the future someone tries to organize

671
00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:29.333
a 51% attack,

672
00:26:29.333 --> 00:26:31.458
some jurisdictions' laws may deem it illegal,

673
00:26:31.458 --> 00:26:32.791
or those people may face large class actions.

674
00:26:32.791 --> 00:26:35.333
So the money they make might not cover the damages.

675
00:26:35.333 --> 00:26:37.958
So theoretically it's possible,

676
00:26:37.958 --> 00:26:38.708
but in practice

677
00:26:38.708 --> 00:26:42.291
it's only theoretically possible — just a small-probability event,

678
00:26:42.291 --> 00:26:43.291
maybe impossible.

679
00:26:43.291 --> 00:26:45.208
Let's return to the miner question.

680
00:26:45.208 --> 00:26:47.166
If miners earn less than Bitcoin treasury companies,

681
00:26:47.166 --> 00:26:48.416
and everyone prefers the simpler route,

682
00:26:48.416 --> 00:26:49.875
with fewer miners over time,

683
00:26:49.875 --> 00:26:52.291
what impact does that have on the Bitcoin network?

684
00:26:52.291 --> 00:26:55.625
Miners won't necessarily decrease endlessly.

685
00:26:55.625 --> 00:26:57.625
I said the number of Bitcoin treasury companies that can exist is small.

686
00:26:57.625 --> 00:27:01.083
But there are many people who can mine.

687
00:27:01.083 --> 00:27:04.416
Those two groups don't overlap much.

688
00:27:04.416 --> 00:27:07.000
So one increasing doesn't make the other decrease.

689
00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:08.208
As I said,

690
00:27:08.208 --> 00:27:09.833
there may be only dozens of listed Bitcoin treasury companies worldwide,

691
00:27:09.833 --> 00:27:11.416
or maybe a few hundred now,

692
00:27:11.416 --> 00:27:14.625
but the ones that can do it well are only a few dozen.

693
00:27:14.625 --> 00:27:17.333
Yet mining can be done by tens of thousands, even hundreds of thousands.

694
00:27:17.333 --> 00:27:19.250
Anyone can mine — just plug in and mine.

695
00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:21.041
But nowadays mining needs some results;

696
00:27:21.041 --> 00:27:21.750
you need scale.

697
00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:22.958
That's how it is.

698
00:27:22.958 --> 00:27:24.375
Mining is dynamically adjusted.

699
00:27:24.375 --> 00:27:26.958
When returns are high, anyone can mine.

700
00:27:26.958 --> 00:27:29.625
Even with one-dollar electricity you can mine.

701
00:27:29.625 --> 00:27:32.458
When returns are low, only cheap electricity makes it viable.

702
00:27:32.458 --> 00:27:34.916
If your power is expensive, you can't mine.

703
00:27:34.916 --> 00:27:36.708
Now back to Ethereum treasury companies.

704
00:27:36.708 --> 00:27:38.208
Ethereum treasury companies are a hot topic.

705
00:27:38.208 --> 00:27:40.666
Tom Lee, right?

706
00:27:40.666 --> 00:27:42.083
Many say because it's PoS,

707
00:27:42.083 --> 00:27:44.166
if too much Ethereum is concentrated in a few companies,

708
00:27:44.166 --> 00:27:46.291
that could be dangerous for the network.

709
00:27:46.291 --> 00:27:48.541
What do you think?

710
00:27:48.541 --> 00:27:51.208
Theoretically yes — like Bitcoin's 51% attack.

711
00:27:51.208 --> 00:27:53.958
If all the hashpower is concentrated in two or three entities,

712
00:27:53.958 --> 00:27:55.791
they could launch an attack.

713
00:27:55.791 --> 00:27:58.000
Even two or three mining pools together could do it.

714
00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:00.291
But the same applies to companies like Tom Lee's.

715
00:28:00.291 --> 00:28:03.666
It's like Alibaba, Tencent, Google, Amazon — control is with the companies,

716
00:28:03.666 --> 00:28:05.625
they could theoretically act against users,

717
00:28:05.625 --> 00:28:06.166
right?

718
00:28:06.166 --> 00:28:07.500
So in reality,

719
00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:09.333
nobody would go and attack.

720
00:28:09.333 --> 00:28:11.500
That reasoning makes sense.

721
00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:12.625
If Tom Lee wanted to attack,

722
00:28:12.625 --> 00:28:14.083
other board members wouldn't agree.

723
00:28:14.083 --> 00:28:16.083
Management wouldn't agree either, right?

724
00:28:16.083 --> 00:28:19.166
So it's basically impossible.

725
00:28:19.166 --> 00:28:21.333
Because if these companies hoard Ethereum

726
00:28:21.333 --> 00:28:22.666
and attack, then...

727
00:28:22.666 --> 00:28:24.666
Of course, how much do you hoard?

728
00:28:24.666 --> 00:28:26.958
If you hoard over 20% of Ethereum,

729
00:28:26.958 --> 00:28:29.375
theoretically you could control it.

730
00:28:29.375 --> 00:28:31.666
Would your company then be considered an investment company?

731
00:28:31.666 --> 00:28:35.333
Would that form change and bring other problems?

732
00:28:35.333 --> 00:28:37.958
If you hoard little, you're just an allocator, right?

733
00:28:37.958 --> 00:28:41.500
Now, to the young viewers of our show:

734
00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:42.625
If you haven't seriously started storing Bitcoin,

735
00:28:42.625 --> 00:28:44.083
is it too late?

736
00:28:44.083 --> 00:28:46.083
I don't think it's late — it's still early.

737
00:28:46.083 --> 00:28:47.625
How so?

738
00:28:47.625 --> 00:28:48.416
Like I said,

739
00:28:48.416 --> 00:28:51.166
we believe Bitcoin is the anchor for the future digital world

740
00:28:51.166 --> 00:28:53.541
and the AI-driven world.

741
00:28:53.541 --> 00:28:55.916
If you believe the AI-powered digital world

742
00:28:55.916 --> 00:28:57.416
will be bigger than the internet world,

743
00:28:57.416 --> 00:29:01.083
most people accept that.

744
00:29:01.083 --> 00:29:03.083
The internet era was bigger than the industrial revolution era.

745
00:29:03.083 --> 00:29:04.666
The industrial era's anchor was gold, right?

746
00:29:04.666 --> 00:29:05.916
So in the future digital/AI era,

747
00:29:05.916 --> 00:29:07.416
the world's anchor will be Bitcoin.

748
00:29:07.416 --> 00:29:08.833
This world will be larger

749
00:29:08.833 --> 00:29:11.875
than the gold-based industrial revolution era.

750
00:29:11.875 --> 00:29:14.041
So Bitcoin exceeding gold in price—

751
00:29:14.041 --> 00:29:17.333
Bitcoin exceeding $1,000,000 is just the starting point,

752
00:29:17.333 --> 00:29:18.666
not the end.

753
00:29:18.666 --> 00:29:19.958
Right — in the long term,

754
00:29:19.958 --> 00:29:21.708
from a 10–20 year perspective,

755
00:29:21.708 --> 00:29:23.375
that's how I understand it.

756
00:29:23.375 --> 00:29:27.125
Also, compared to gold, Bitcoin has three advantages.

757
00:29:27.125 --> 00:29:28.583
One,

758
00:29:28.583 --> 00:29:31.458
it has a global user network effect.

759
00:29:31.458 --> 00:29:32.875
Because gold is local,

760
00:29:32.875 --> 00:29:35.166
gold in each place is independent and different.

761
00:29:35.166 --> 00:29:36.250
Second, it aggregates global liquidity,

762
00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:37.666
because you're buying the same Bitcoin.

763
00:29:37.666 --> 00:29:39.041
When you sell gold,

764
00:29:39.041 --> 00:29:42.041
you might take a 10% discount or more — big slippage, right?

765
00:29:42.041 --> 00:29:42.833
Third, on Bitcoin

766
00:29:42.833 --> 00:29:44.791
—like at today’s Bitcoin Conference —

767
00:29:44.791 --> 00:29:47.000
there are many Bitcoin-ecosystem tech and fintech projects,

768
00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:47.958
showing Bitcoin has tech attributes.

769
00:29:47.958 --> 00:29:50.166
So from a future macro perspective,

770
00:29:50.166 --> 00:29:52.958
and its comparison to gold,

771
00:29:52.958 --> 00:29:55.000
Bitcoin has a better chance.

772
00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.666
When will Bitcoin become a traditional asset

773
00:29:57.666 --> 00:30:00.041
with no major growth potential?

774
00:30:00.041 --> 00:30:02.500
I think that will be when it's long-term mature,

775
00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:04.166
like when you have children.

776
00:30:04.166 --> 00:30:05.708
As Chinese people give gold as gifts at weddings,

777
00:30:05.708 --> 00:30:08.250
when you give your child some Bitcoin at their wedding,

778
00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:10.333
and everyone thinks that way,

779
00:30:10.333 --> 00:30:11.833
that means it's a very mature asset.

780
00:31:18.291 --> 00:31:20.458
Up until now, buying Bitcoin has been the right decision.

781
00:31:20.458 --> 00:31:21.958
It still feels like it's a long way off.

782
00:31:21.958 --> 00:31:23.250
I don't know how long.

783
00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:24.541
Maybe 10 years, maybe 20 years.

784
00:31:24.541 --> 00:31:28.250
So maybe we shouldn't promote it too aggressively.

785
00:31:28.250 --> 00:31:29.500
I have another question.

786
00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:32.875
My brain literally froze just now.

787
00:31:32.875 --> 00:31:34.666
I just heard the “one million” line.

788
00:31:34.666 --> 00:31:35.583
“One million is only the beginning.”

789
00:31:35.583 --> 00:31:37.208
My mind is totally frozen.

790
00:31:37.208 --> 00:31:38.416
You too, right?

791
00:31:38.416 --> 00:31:39.500
Rush out and buy coins, right?

792
00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:42.375
Yes — I just saw our producer stand up.

793
00:31:42.375 --> 00:31:45.000
You went to buy one just now.

794
00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:47.625
“One million is the starting point.”

795
00:31:47.625 --> 00:31:48.791
Yeah — can I think about it this way:

796
00:31:48.791 --> 00:31:50.833
Many so-called Bitcoin whales

797
00:31:50.833 --> 00:31:53.208
got in very, very early.

798
00:31:53.208 --> 00:31:55.000
Like OGs who hoarded lots of Bitcoin early on.

799
00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:57.750
And many of us who joined the crypto space

800
00:31:57.750 --> 00:32:01.416
actually hoped to make wealth that flips our social class.

801
00:32:01.416 --> 00:32:04.875
But is this just a repeat of traditional finance—

802
00:32:04.875 --> 00:32:07.291
the rich get richer?

803
00:32:07.291 --> 00:32:09.166
You come in now

804
00:32:09.166 --> 00:32:12.291
and you buy one that costs, say, 100,000.

805
00:32:12.291 --> 00:32:14.291
Although in the long run it's still cheap,

806
00:32:14.291 --> 00:32:16.083
you just can't buy that many.

807
00:32:16.083 --> 00:32:18.500
In theory,

808
00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:20.583
you see in our era many people —

809
00:32:20.583 --> 00:32:24.083
including many former executives at our company —

810
00:32:24.083 --> 00:32:25.583
held over 10,000 BTC, right?

811
00:32:25.583 --> 00:32:27.916
They might have used tens of thousands of BTC

812
00:32:27.916 --> 00:32:29.166
to buy a car,

813
00:32:29.166 --> 00:32:30.750
or tens of thousands to buy a house.

814
00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:32.958
So you find many people

815
00:32:32.958 --> 00:32:34.125
when they first owned Bitcoin

816
00:32:34.125 --> 00:32:36.041
they held the largest amounts at the lowest prices.

817
00:32:36.041 --> 00:32:37.625
Many people.

818
00:32:37.625 --> 00:32:39.750
It's hard to own more Bitcoin now than you did back then.

819
00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:41.791
Because if you had BTC at $1,

820
00:32:41.791 --> 00:32:43.500
when it rose to $100 you wouldn't buy more.

821
00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:46.458
And along the way you may have sold a lot of Bitcoin.

822
00:32:46.458 --> 00:32:49.333
So actually the world is fair to everyone:

823
00:32:49.333 --> 00:32:51.166
it's not about when you joined the industry,

824
00:32:51.166 --> 00:32:54.333
it's about when you gained the awareness.

825
00:32:54.333 --> 00:32:56.458
If you had that awareness 10 years ago,

826
00:32:56.458 --> 00:32:57.791
you'd now certainly be —

827
00:32:57.791 --> 00:33:00.708
not necessarily extremely rich, but financially free, right?

828
00:33:00.708 --> 00:33:03.666
Yet many early participants are now back to poverty

829
00:33:03.666 --> 00:33:06.333
because they no longer hold Bitcoin, right?

830
00:33:06.333 --> 00:33:09.000
Of course, the top figures you see —

831
00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:11.291
like Zhao Changpeng in Hong Kong these past two days,

832
00:33:11.291 --> 00:33:13.666
Huobi founder Li Lin,

833
00:33:13.666 --> 00:33:15.541
OKX’s Xu Mingxing,

834
00:33:15.541 --> 00:33:17.708
and several leading mining-hardware companies like Bitmain —

835
00:33:17.708 --> 00:33:19.416
they've stayed in the space.

836
00:33:19.416 --> 00:33:22.625
They likely remained long-term bullish on Bitcoin, so they survived;

837
00:33:22.625 --> 00:33:23.250
otherwise they would have pivoted.

838
00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:24.125
Speaking of OKX’s Xu Mingxing,

839
00:33:24.125 --> 00:33:25.791
he once said in a talk:

840
00:33:25.791 --> 00:33:27.125
If you call yourself a KOL

841
00:33:27.125 --> 00:33:28.416
and you don't have five or ten Bitcoins,

842
00:33:28.416 --> 00:33:30.333
don't call yourself a KOL.

843
00:33:30.333 --> 00:33:32.333
My curious question is:

844
00:33:32.333 --> 00:33:34.000
If an ordinary person

845
00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:36.000
wants to say they're a comfortably well-off middle-class person

846
00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:36.916
—meaning not buying things without ever checking prices—

847
00:33:36.916 --> 00:33:38.333
how many Bitcoins do you need?

848
00:33:38.333 --> 00:33:40.916
If you allocate 10%–20% of your current assets to Bitcoin,

849
00:33:40.916 --> 00:33:43.500
in ten years you won't have to check prices when buying things.

850
00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:45.750
Based on your current lifestyle,

851
00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:46.833
you won't have to watch prices when shopping.

852
00:33:46.833 --> 00:33:49.416
You're not thinking of buying private jets or huge yachts now,

853
00:33:49.416 --> 00:33:50.541
so it depends on how much you spend.

854
00:33:50.541 --> 00:33:52.958
Right now many people might splurge on a nicer fruit

855
00:33:52.958 --> 00:33:55.291
or a better bottle of wine and consider the price,

856
00:33:55.291 --> 00:33:58.875
or not have it every day. But ten years from now, with that Bitcoin,

857
00:33:58.875 --> 00:34:01.750
you won't need to worry about these prices when choosing.

858
00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:04.625
He kept nodding vigorously.

859
00:34:04.625 --> 00:34:05.416
I think that's great.

860
00:34:05.416 --> 00:34:07.041
I'm really so happy with this episode.

861
00:34:07.041 --> 00:34:09.333
To hear so many wise words,

862
00:34:09.333 --> 00:34:10.791
from a Bitcoin OG

863
00:34:10.791 --> 00:34:13.291
who also took a company public,

864
00:34:13.291 --> 00:34:14.375
did mining,

865
00:34:14.375 --> 00:34:16.625
and now works on DATs —

866
00:34:16.625 --> 00:34:20.291
I think your resume is very complete,

867
00:34:20.291 --> 00:34:22.625
and I believe the audience will learn a lot.

868
00:34:22.625 --> 00:34:23.250
Thank you for coming today.

869
00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:24.125
Thank you.

870
00:34:24.125 --> 00:34:25.791
Hope you can come again.

871
00:34:25.791 --> 00:34:27.125
Thanks.

872
00:34:27.125 --> 00:34:28.416
Did the audience, after hearing this episode,

873
00:34:28.416 --> 00:34:30.500
go place more Bitcoin orders?

874
00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:34.458
After listening I really want to buy more quickly.

875
00:34:34.458 --> 00:34:36.750
He already placed an order.

876
00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:37.666
Thank you.

877
00:34:37.666 --> 00:34:38.625
Thanks.
